@@ -1,241 +0,0 @@
-# Trees
-
-Grow a tree from a seed. Pick a species, roll the dice until the shape is right, and take away a game-ready mesh with bark UVs, foliage cards and wind data already baked in. Nothing is sculpted and nothing is downloaded — the whole tree comes out of about two kilobytes of numbers.
-
-Trees are a **workspace of their own**, reached from **Trees** in the top navigation.
-
-> Generation runs on the **Python mesh-tools service**. Configure it in **Settings → Mesh Tools**; the desktop app starts it for you.
-
-## The tree is the spec
-
-This is the one idea worth understanding before anything else, because everything on the page follows from it.
-
-A tree is described by a **TreeSpec**: a small JSON document holding a seed and a few dozen numbers. That document *is* the tree. Feed the same spec in twice and you get the same mesh back, triangle for triangle. The GLB is not the asset — it is an output the spec can produce again at any time.
-
-That is what makes the page's main loop possible:
-
-- **Re-rolling costs nothing.** A new seed is a new tree, not an edit to an old one.
-- **Nothing is destructive.** Every slider can go back where it was.
-- **Saving a tree saves the spec.** It rides in the mesh asset's metadata, so a tree you made months ago can be reopened, re-rolled or nudged without keeping the mesh around.
-- **Specs are files.** **Export spec** writes a `.tree.json` you can commit, diff, mail to someone, or drop into a build script.
-
-## Two speeds
-
-The page deliberately runs at two speeds, and knowing which is which explains why it feels the way it does.
-
-**Moving a slider** regrows only the **skeleton** — the branch centre-lines, drawn as plain lines. It takes about a tenth of a second, so it can keep up while you drag.
-
-**Generate mesh** builds the real thing: swept bark, foliage cards, UVs, materials. It takes about a second.
-
-So the cheap thing is live and the expensive thing is a button. When a parameter that moves branches changes, the mesh on screen is dropped and you are back to the skeleton — because a mesh built from the previous numbers would be showing you something the panel no longer says.
-
-## Tree presets — saving your parameters
-
-**Species** presets ship with the app and are read-only. Your own saved trees are **Tree Presets**, a first-class asset type: they live in the asset library alongside images and meshes, with a thumbnail, a name, tags and search.
-
-**Save preset…** stores the current parameter set. **Open preset…** picks one back through the ordinary asset browser. They also appear under **Assets → Tree Presets**, where you can rename, tag, filter and delete them like anything else.
-
-What gets saved is the *spec*: every slider, the seed, and the ids of the images in the texture slots — about two kilobytes. The mesh is not saved, because it does not need to be; the spec rebuilds it exactly. (**Save to library** is the separate action that stores the mesh itself.)
-
-### The thumbnail
-
-Saving always renders one, even if the tree has no textures and even if you never pressed **Generate mesh** — an untextured tree still thumbnails to a recognisable shape, and a shape is what tells two presets apart in a grid. If a preset is saved without a mesh to hand, one is generated for the picture and then discarded.
-
-### Textures are references
-
-The preset records *which* images it used, not copies of them. One bark photograph can dress fifty trees without fifty copies of it, and re-saving a texture updates every preset that points at it.
-
-The consequence is worth knowing: an image you **uploaded from disk** into a texture slot cannot be referenced, because its bytes only ever existed in that browser tab. Its slot is saved empty. Pick textures from the asset library if you want them to come back. An image deleted from the library since the save also comes back empty, and loading says how many slots that happened to — better than a broken thumbnail.
-
-**Export spec** still writes the same document to a `.tree.json` file. Use presets for day-to-day work and export when you want to commit a tree to source control or send it to someone.
-
-## Species
-
-Ten presets ship: **oak**, **pine**, **birch**, **poplar**, **willow**, **palm**, **bush**, **bonsai**, **sapling** and **dead**. Start from one. They are not decoration — a tree built from raw sliders almost always looks wrong, and each preset is a tuned set of about twenty values that a species actually needs.
-
-Switching species **keeps your seed**, so you can grow an oak and a pine from the same number and compare them.
-
-## How a tree is grown
-
-Worth a paragraph, because it explains what the parameters do.
-
-Branches are grown by **space colonization**. A cloud of *attraction points* is scattered inside the crown envelope; each point pulls the nearest branch tip toward it, and a tip that gets close enough consumes it. Branches therefore **compete** for the same space, which is what makes the result read as grown rather than drawn — a branch thins out where a neighbour already took the light.
-
-The consequences you can steer:
-
-| You want | Turn |
-| --- | --- |
-| A different silhouette | **Crown shape** — the single biggest lever |
-| More or fewer branches | **Branch density** |
-| A weeping tree | **Gravitropism** negative (Advanced) |
-| Gnarled, storybook branches | **Randomness** up (Advanced) |
-| Finer twigs | **Branch law exponent** down (Advanced) |
-
-Branch thickness is not a slider per branch: it follows **da Vinci's rule**, where a limb is as thick as the limbs growing out of it, so a trunk is always consistent with the crown it carries.
-
-## Parameters
-
-Eight controls are visible by default. Roughly forty more sit behind **Advanced parameters**, grouped into **Skeleton**, **Branching**, **Foliage** and **Output**. Most trees never need them; the presets carry that complexity for you. Every control has a tooltip explaining what it does.
-
-Sizes are **ratios of the tree's height**, not absolute measurements. Change **Height** alone and you get the same tree, bigger — nothing needs re-tuning.
-
-## Foliage
-
-Leaves are **cards** — flat quads carrying a leaf texture — and there are two things to know about them.
-
-### The leaf budget is a cap, not a target
-
-**Leaf budget** can only ever *remove* cards; it cannot create them. The number of leaves actually placed comes from how many spots on the tree qualify — set by **Leaf spacing** (how far apart placements sit along a branch) and **Max host radius** (how thick a branch may be and still carry leaves).
-
-So raising the budget past the number of available spots does nothing at all. The **Stats** panel says which constraint bound: *capped by budget* means the budget is doing the work and lowering it will thin the tree; *capped by placements* means the budget is slack and you want **Leaf spacing** down or **Max host radius** up instead.
-
-**Max host radius** is a fraction of the **trunk** radius, not of the tree's height. That matters: every branch scales with the trunk, so a height-relative threshold silently gets stricter as the trunk thickens — at a very thick trunk almost nothing qualified, the canopy thinned out, and the leaf budget appeared to stop working entirely.
-
-**Clusters, not single cards.** A cluster is a small fan of cards placed as one unit. It reads as a volume of leaves from any angle for a fraction of the cost, and it is the default because a leaf-per-twig tree runs to tens of thousands of cards and is unusable in an engine. **Leaf budget** is a hard cap: go over it and placements are thinned *evenly across the tree*, never chopped off one side.
-
-**Normals are doing the work, not shape.** A flat card lit by its own flat normal looks like cardboard, and a canopy of them looks like a pile of cardboard. Leaf normals are instead pointed **outward from the crown centre**, so the canopy shades like the rounded mass it stands for. That single trick is most of why the foliage reads as foliage. It is the **Spherical normals** slider, and there is very rarely a reason to lower it.
-
-For palms and conifers, **Align to branch** turns a leaf sticking out sideways into a frond running *along* its branch. A palm needs it near 1.
-
-## Bark and UVs
-
-Branches are swept tubes with **analytic cylindrical UVs** — the horizontal coordinate is the angle around the branch, the vertical one is distance along it. That is an exact answer rather than an approximation, so bark tiles perfectly and there is no reason to run **Auto UV** on a tree. **Bark UV tile** sets how much world length one tile of the texture covers.
-
-### Junctions
-
-Where a branch meets its parent, the two tubes simply **overlap** and the bark texture hides the join. It is free, it looks right, and it keeps the UVs exact.
-
-If you need a genuinely **closed solid** — 3D printing, physics cooking, volume queries — switch **Junctions** to **Clean**. Every branch is boolean-unioned into one watertight surface. It costs about a second and the UVs are re-derived rather than exact, so use it when you need the solid and leave it alone when you do not.
-
-## Textures
-
-The **Textures** panel on the right has three slots. Each takes an image from your asset library (**Choose…**) or straight off disk (**Upload**); click a thumbnail to remove it.
-
-| Slot | What it takes |
-| --- | --- |
-| **Trunk** | One tileable bark image. It repeats along every branch — **Bark UV tile** sets how much world length one repeat covers. |
-| **Branches** | Optional. A second bark image for the thin wood. |
-| **Leaves** | One *or more* leaf cut-outs, **with alpha**. |
-
-### Leaves take a list, not an atlas
-
-The renderer wants an *atlas* — one image holding a grid of leaf cut-outs that each card indexes into. That is the wrong thing to ask you for, because nobody has an atlas lying around; you have a few leaf PNGs. So hand over the PNGs and the generator builds the grid, fits each leaf into its cell without stretching it, and tells the cards how many cells actually hold a leaf.
-
-That last part matters more than it sounds. Three leaves need a 2×2 grid, which leaves one cell empty, and a card that sampled the empty cell would alpha-test away and punch a hole in the canopy. The generator counts the real tiles and only draws from those, so any number of leaves works.
-
-### Where the leaf attaches — the pivot
-
-A card attaches to its branch at one point on the leaf image and hangs downward from there. That point is the **pivot**, and you set it: click the crosshair on a leaf thumbnail to open the image full size, then click where the stem meets the branch. A live preview beside it shows how the leaf will sit once it is on a twig.
-
-Only a point is needed, not an angle — a leaf hangs away from its own attachment, so the direction is implied by where you clicked.
-
-**The pivot is stored on the tree preset, not written into the image.** The same leaf photo can be shared by several trees, and each is free to hang it differently.
-
-When you add leaves, a detector places a first guess so you are nudging a point rather than starting from nothing. It finds the stem as the most *slender* protrusion from the blade — not the longest, which on a pointed leaf is the tip. It is right often enough to be worth having and wrong often enough to be worth correcting, which is exactly why the point is editable.
-
-### Which way up is a leaf?
-
-A card attaches to its branch along the **top edge** of its atlas tile and hangs downward from there — so a leaf image wants its stem at the top.
-
-Generated cut-outs never come that way: ask an image model for a leaf and you get one at whatever angle it felt like, stem anywhere. **Auto-orient leaves** (on by default) finds the stem in each image and rotates it upright before it goes into the atlas.
-
-It finds the stem geometrically, as the most *slender* protrusion from the blade — not the longest one, which on a pointed leaf is the tip, and not the palest, because a stalk is sometimes darker than its own blade. Turn it off if you are supplying a hand-authored atlas that is already laid out correctly.
-
-Three or four variants is plenty — with random per-card tile choice, rotation and tilt, a handful reads as a whole canopy.
-
-### Branches are optional on purpose
-
-Leave the **Branches** slot empty and the whole tree wears the trunk texture as **one bark material and one draw call**. Fill it and the bark splits in two, which costs a draw call — so it never happens behind your back. The split is by thickness: **Bark → split radius** (Advanced) sets how thin wood has to be before it counts as a branch.
-
-### Generated from a prompt
-
-These are ordinary image assets, so you can **generate them**. Produce a tileable bark from a species prompt and leaf cut-outs on a transparent background with the image tools, then pick them here — or pass them to the MCP tool as `trunkTextureAssetId`, `branchTextureAssetId` and `leafImageAssetIds`.
-
-Procedural structure with generated surface is the combination worth reaching for: the shape is exact and reproducible, and the species is whatever you can describe.
-
-> Leaf images need real transparency. A leaf on a white background comes out as a leaf on a **white card**, because the alpha channel is what cuts the card down to the leaf.
-
-## Wind data
-
-**Bake wind data** writes four values per vertex into the mesh's vertex colours, ready for a wind shader in Unity, Unreal or Godot:
-
-| Channel | Meaning |
-| --- | --- |
-| **R** | Branch phase — a per-branch offset so limbs do not sway in unison |
-| **G** | Stiffness — 1 at the trunk, falling to 0 at the tips |
-| **B** | Leaf flutter phase — per-card randomness |
-| **A** | Hierarchy weight — how deep in the tree this vertex sits |
-
-**It is off by default, and the reason is worth knowing.** glTF defines vertex colour as a *multiplier on base colour*, so any spec-compliant viewer paints the tree with this data: the bark comes out green (its blue channel is always zero) and the foliage comes out as confetti. The numbers are correct — they are simply not colours. The Trees viewport ignores the tint either way, so the preview always shows you the material.
-
-Turn it on when the tree is headed for an engine wind shader that samples vertex colour as data, and expect it to look wrong in Blender, in a glTF viewer, and in this app's other mesh views.
-
-## Output
-
-The pivot sits at the **base of the trunk**, which is where a scatter tool or a level editor expects to find it. **Engine preset** applies the axis and unit convention for your target — Unreal, for instance, means centimetres and Z-up.
-
-**Download GLB** saves the mesh. **Download GLB Variants** asks how many you want, generates that many with a new random seed each, and delivers them as one zip — named by seed, with a `variants.json` recording the base spec and every seed, so any file in the set can be reproduced or reopened later. It is one archive rather than several downloads because browsers treat repeated automatic downloads as suspicious and start blocking them. Textures are loaded once and reused across the batch, and the button becomes **Cancel variants** while it runs — cancelling still downloads whatever finished.
-
-**Download GLB** saves the mesh. **Save to library** stores it as a mesh asset with its spec attached; trees are global, like assemblies, and belong to no project, because a tree routinely mixes a bark texture from one project with a leaf atlas from another.
-
-Saving also **renders a thumbnail**, so the tree is recognisable in the asset library rather than one more identical placeholder icon — and the same image is attached to the saved parameter set, so **My trees** shows what each entry actually grows. If the thumbnail fails to render the mesh is still saved; it is cosmetic, and losing a save over a picture would be the wrong trade.
-
-## LODs and impostors
-
-**Download LODs** builds a whole distance chain in one go and delivers it as a zip: `Tree_LOD0.glb`, `Tree_LOD1.glb` and so on, plus a `lods.json` recording what each level cost. Set how many under **Advanced → Output → LOD levels**.
-
-### Regenerated, not simplified
-
-A tree is the worst possible input for a general mesh simplifier. Decimation collapses edges that barely change the surface — but a canopy is thousands of separate alpha-tested quads, so there are no edges to collapse that do not delete whole leaves, and the silhouette (the only thing that matters at distance) is the first thing to go.
-
-So the levels are not simplified, they are **rebuilt** — fewer sides per branch, whole twig generations dropped, and fewer but proportionally larger leaf cards. A typical oak comes out around 100% → 64% → 42% → 19% of its triangles with the shape intact.
-
-The rule that makes it work: **the skeleton never changes between levels.** Every level is a different skin over identical branch centre lines, so a branch that exists at two levels is in exactly the same place and the same thickness in both. Re-growing the tree with lighter settings would swap one tree for a different tree at the distance threshold, which is the most visible popping there is.
-
-Card size grows as levels drop cards, but only up to **twice** the LOD0 size. Past roughly that, a card stops reading as a leaf and starts reading as a slab — and because a card grows outward from the branch it hangs on, oversized cards visibly inflate the crown. The cap is what keeps the silhouette within a few percent of LOD0 all the way down the chain.
-
-Each level keeps a **fraction** of the tree's branch-order range rather than dropping a fixed number of generations. How deep a skeleton goes is a property of the tree — a sparse oak reaches order 5, a densely branched one reaches 12 — so subtracting one order per level cuts almost nothing on the trees that need LODs most.
-
-Every lever bottoms out eventually: branch sides reach their floor of three, leaf cards reach their size cap, and the order cull runs out of orders. **A level that would cost more than three quarters of the one before it is dropped**, because it is a file nobody would ever switch to and one more LOD transition for nothing. Ask for five levels on a tree that only supports three and you get three, numbered contiguously.
-
-How many levels are worth asking for depends on the tree. Each level culls one branch generation, and a typical oak's skeleton is only five deep — so a 4-level chain is culling down to the first fork by the end, and LOD4 is necessarily sparse. Two or three levels plus an impostor is usually the better shape than four levels.
-
-Card size is tied to card count for the same reason. Coverage goes as count × width², so when a level drops to 42% of the cards their width grows by 1/√0.42 — the canopy keeps filling the same silhouette instead of visibly thinning as levels swap.
-
-### Impostors
-
-Tick **Bake impostor** and the archive also gets a hemi-octahedral impostor: a grid of pre-rendered views (8×8 by default, so 64), an albedo and a normal atlas, a quad, and a `.json` describing the mapping.
-
-At real forest distances a tree is a few dozen pixels, and even the cheapest LOD is thousands of triangles spent on something smaller than a thumbnail. An impostor replaces the geometry entirely: the shader picks the views nearest the camera direction and blends them, so the billboard turns convincingly as you walk around it. This is what makes a forest of thousands of trees affordable.
-
-The atlas is baked from **LOD0**, the full-detail tree — not from the cheapest level. The impostor replaces the geometry outright at distance, so its silhouette *is* the tree there, and baking from a thinned level would freeze that thinning into the atlas permanently.
-
-**Impostor quality** is a supersampling rate. Every sample that survives the depth test is averaged into its pixel, so it controls antialiasing as well as coverage — too low and the atlas comes out grainier than the leaf texture it was baked from, which reads as speckle across the canopy.
-
-The bake is CPU work and its cost follows the **atlas area** (views per axis × view size, squared) times the tree's triangle count. A 1024px atlas of a full-detail oak takes around 20 seconds; a 2048px one takes about a minute. Doubling the view size quadruples the bake, so raise it deliberately.
-
-*Hemi*-octahedral because a tree is rooted to the ground and never seen from below — spending half the atlas on views nobody looks at would halve the resolution of the ones they do.
-
-> The quad in the GLB is UV'd to one near-horizontal view, so it shows something sensible in an ordinary glTF viewer. **View-dependent sampling needs an impostor shader on the engine side** — the `.json` gives it the grid size, the mapping and the bounds. The normals are stored in view space.
-
-## Automation
-
-The MCP server exposes the generator as `list_tree_presets`, `preview_tree_skeleton` and `generate_tree` — see **[MCP](39.md)**. The same generator also runs from the command line for batch work:
-
-```
-python -m app.services.treegen --preset oak --seed 7 -o oak.glb
-python -m app.services.treegen --all -o forest/
-python -m app.services.treegen --verify
-```
-
-## When a tree comes out wrong
-
-**A bare pole with no branches.** The crown is out of reach. Raise **Attraction distance** or lower **Trunk clearance** so the trunk's tip can sense the crown.
-
-**A shrub with no trunk.** **Trunk clearance** is near zero — which is exactly what the *bush* preset wants, and rarely what anything else does.
-
-**Branches too coarse and blunt.** Lower **Growth step** (Advanced) for finer branches, and lower the **Branch law exponent** for finer twigs. Both cost generation time.
-
-**A dense green ball with no visible structure.** The foliage is winning. Lower the **Leaf budget** or **Leaf size**, or press **Show skeleton** to check the branches underneath are actually good.
-
-**Generation is slow.** **Branch density** and **Growth step** dominate the cost. The skeleton is capped so it cannot run away, but a tree asked for twenty thousand attraction points at a fine step will take its time.
